  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAn upper-level shortwave will be moving over SD early this morning. \nAs this shortwave moves over, a warm front will be pushing through \nwestern and central SD. This warm front, in combination with a low \nlevel jet pushing moisture into central SD and support from the \nuppre-level shortwave, could cause some scattered showers and storms \nto develop this morning. The high-res models do vary the location of \nthese storms, as some of them have the storms moving through south \ncentral SD while others keep them to the south. These storms have a \nmarginal risk (level 1 of 5) to become severe if something were to \ndevelop. This is because of the shear available from the low level \njet as well as some stronger mid-level lapse rates, which could \ncause 1 inch hail and 60 mph wind gusts to get to the surface. \nThrough the morning, the low level jet will dissipate, and the storm \nthreat also decreases. Temperatures will warm slightly behind the \nwarm front moving through. Highs today are expected to be in the 80s \nto mid 90s, with the warmest temperatures over areas west of the \nMissouri river. The wildfire smoke will continue to move in over SD \nalong the upper-level northwest flow for the coming days. This smoke \nlooks to continue to stay aloft over central and northeastern SD and \ncause hazy skies. \n\nAnother upper-level shortwave is forecast to move over SD again \nduring the evening into Sunday morning as the models are showing a \nfrontal boundary and/or a surface low moving over northeastern SD. \nThe warmer temperatures through the day will help to build the \ninstability over central and northeastern SD, with some models \nshowing 1000-2000 J/kg of CAPE over the area by tonight. This \ninstability, in combination with a front/surface low moving through, \nwill cause some scattered showers and storms to develop late this \nafternoon into Sunday morning. The models vary how much and where \nmoisture might move in over the area, which does lead to a \ndifference in the models as to where and when storms might develop. \nIn addition to the moisture, the models also show a stronger CAP \ndeveloping over central and northeastern SD, which would limit the \ndevelopment of storms. Some models have the storms developing in \nnorth central SD during the evening where there is less of a CAP and \nthen moving southeast across the area while other models will have \nsome storms break through the CAP in northeastern SD during the \novernight hours. Then during the overnight hours, another low level \njet will be developing and will help to provide shear to support the \nstorms over northeastern SD. Additionally, there will be some fairly \ndecent mid-level lapse rates, which will help to support the \ndevelopment of large hail and strong winds. There is a Marginal risk \n(level 1 of 5) for these storms to become severe if one develops. \nThe main hazards will be for wind gusts of 60 mph and hail of 1 inch \nor more in diameter. \n\nCooler temperatures will be moving in behind the surface low Sunday \nand into next week. High temperatures will be in the upper 70s to \nupper 80s. Models still vary the chances for shower and storm Sunday \nnight into Monday as the chances for storms to actually occur have \ndecreased through the previous model runs. The showers/storms look \nto develop along a surface boundary in WY/MT Sunday evening. Then \nthey will move east through the evening overnight into Monday \nmorning. The models vary in how far these showers and storms make it \nbefore they dissipate, with some models having the storms move \nthrough ND, some have them get into western SD, other have the \nstorms move through central and northeastern SD, and other models \nhave the storms die off before getting anywhere close to the area. \nThis has lowered confidence in showers and storms developing, but \nchances still remain between 30-50% chance that something moves over \nthe area Sunday night into Monday morning. There is a marginal risk \n(level 1 of 5) for storms to become severe if they develop, with the \nmain hazard of 1 inch hail and 60 mph wind gusts. Evening and \novernight precipitation chances continue into next week as the upper-\nlevels continue to push shortwaves over SD.",
